# Network configuration for ONE Cisco Nexus 3172TQ. Copy to network.conf.
#
# ⚠ THERE IS NO network.conf UNTIL YOU MAKE ONE, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.
#
# Everything below is this machine's identity: its management address, its
# routing identity, and the addressing of every link it has. None of it is
# true of any other switch. An image built with a network.conf in it carries
# that identity to whatever it is installed on, so a freshly built image
# configures nothing at all and says so at boot.
#
# Two places this can live:
#
#   platform/cisco-n3172tq/config/network.conf   baked into the image you
#                                                build, for a switch you own
#   /mnt/data/config/network.conf                on the switch's own data
#                                                partition, which OVERRIDES
#                                                whatever the image shipped
#
# The second is the better answer for a machine that already runs: it survives
# an upgrade, and an image can then be identical across a fleet.
#
# Syntax: iface <name> <addr/prefix> [mac <mac>|mac auto] [mtu <n>]
#         route <prefix|default> via <gw> [dev <iface>]
#
# The file is re-read and re-applied every 30 seconds until every interface it
# names exists, because the datapath's ports appear minutes after the
# management interface does. Anything already in the state asked for is left
# alone, so re-applying is free.

# ── Management ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠ KEEP `mac auto` AND DO NOT WRITE AN ADDRESS HERE.
#
# This board's NIC EEPROM is blank -- eth0 comes up as 00:a0:c9:00:00:00,
# Intel's OUI with an all-zero suffix -- and that is true of the MODEL, so
# every 3172TQ presents that same address until told otherwise. Writing the
# right one fixes exactly one switch and makes the image wrong for every
# other.
#
# `mac auto` reads the board's own allocated block from its ID PROM, the
# 24c512 at 0x52, and is correct on any unit. See `nosaic platform mac`.
iface eth0 192.0.2.10/24 mac auto
route default via 192.0.2.1

# ── Routing identity ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A stable address that belongs to no single link, so a neighbour losing an
# interface does not lose the router. This is the OSPF router-id; it must be
# unique in the routing domain, and frr.conf has to agree with it.
iface lo 198.51.100.1/32

# ── Front-panel ports ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Names come from the tap_<name> properties in asic.conf, so eth1_31 is panel
# port 31. A port with no address here still exists and still forwards; it
# just has no layer 3.
#
# ⚠ MTU HAS TO MATCH THE NEIGHBOUR. OSPF carries it in its database
# description packets and refuses an adjacency when the two disagree, leaving
# it stuck in ExStart with nothing said about why.
#
# iface eth1_31 203.0.113.1/29 mtu 1600
# iface eth1_53 203.0.113.9/29 mtu 1600
